model {
  extend basalt {
    build = module "Build" {
      // Capacity refusals remain typed across every deploy transport and both web error maps.
      // A timed-out compile replies after its stalled work phase, release and error records;
      // diagnostics wait for that correlated terminal reply before checking the complete trail.
      // Dependency work may resume after bundling; only its last phase and the terminal tail are fixed.
      // Deferred management-app setup sends the onboarding POST before waiting for committed metadata and the user's link and checking inventory once, because GET requests skip dispatch and would leave setup waiting for the recovery cron.
      // Physical database proofs await committed pool accounts and links before seeding synthetic pool metadata.
      // Provisioning readiness wakes on app, account and link commits, not only setup metadata changes.
      description "Dashboard batch profiling prepares the complete E2E assets for each source variant before measuring it, so both variants satisfy the same strict web freshness check. Pool recipe journeys run in the pool CI gate and retain their completed checks and first failure. Managed Cloud pool proofs inject their receiver through the existing composition-root poolOrigin, require exactly one origin declaration and one access-lifecycle wiring in each producer, and restore both saved source files on success or failure. Hook and receiver implementations remain unchanged. Completed proof exports preserve environment and runtime preboot, launch and switch receipts before pruning, so their caller can verify framework reuse and runtime identity. Every E2E upload collects those retained exports, falling back to unpruned original reports when retention is interrupted. Pinned dependency preparation shares immutable package bytes keyed by runtime pin, dependency inputs, toolchain and platform. Each checkout keeps its own carry-modified source and workspace links. Dependency installers copy package bytes into private attempts, preventing Bun-cache hardlinks from exposing published generations to orphan writers. Locks and parent leases bind the PID to the boot identity and exact operating-system process start. Installer groups bind that same incarnation and an inherited nonce, preserving genuine children after the leader exits while rejecting recycled process and group numbers. Live PID-only legacy owners remain authoritative until their process or group exits, preserving mixed-version reader registration. Collection first rejects owners proven expired by boot or start identity, then observes only unresolved lease-bearing groups. It validates all removals before deleting anything and skips the entire pass if current ownership is unverifiable. Allocation first collects and then permits at most three pending attempts per dependency key, refusing further installs with an explicit error while those attempts remain protected. Publication, durable checkout references and collection share one lock; preparation also serializes each checkout across dependency keys. Collection removes abandoned attempts only after their parent and installer group exit, and removes generations only after every checkout releases them. Completion metadata is validated and published atomically, and existing consumers keep their generation through recovery. Prepared-source verification applies the same explicit retired-test removals as declaration preparation; every other missing or unexpected source remains blocking. Connector acceptance children atomically persist each active phase and latest successful assertion count before continuing. Parents retain a bounded output tail projected onto closed diagnostic fields while the child runs; raw output, credentials and session files stay private even after a forced stop. Outside-service OAuth fixture setup supplies one client identity and secret per service before provisioning; transient failures replay the identical credentials within the existing bounded setup budget. Returned credentials must match those inputs, and persistent failures still block setup. Outside-service identity recovery acquires its browser engine during runtime preparation; the bounded engine check launches the prepared binary without network installation. Local emulator HTTP fixtures bind the exact IPv4 loopback origin they advertise, so a competing listener cannot claim that origin. Generated renderer strings follow the pinned code generator exactly and are excluded from source formatting; their dedicated freshness check stays authoritative. Native provider product fixtures serve catalog sources through the production framework mapper. Zendesk maps both basicAuth and oauth2 declarations to the saved OAuth bearer credential; unknown schemes remain unavailable. Catalog lint and connector proofs check the served framework mapping while pinned source snapshots remain unchanged; invalid source pins and local app release mismatches still fail. Pool provisioning fixtures include the organization removal tombstone table, so app writes exercise the normal parent-lock guard. Packaged app renderer bytes and content-hashed static paths are generated together with the pinned build tool after protocol or framework changes. The pool Worker typecheck uses only platform declarations. Shared fetch telemetry preserves browser-only options when present, and redirect policy serializes URL bases before platform parsing. Personal-data reporting scenarios that use organization account APIs and authenticated actors run on hosted targets; the local product has no hosted signup or organization session fixture. Connector sign-in and tool proofs participate in affected selection and the aggregate gate, including changes to their catalog source, loopback OAuth UI and SDK protocol carries. Pull requests default to source, carry and unit checks without heavy suites. Main pushes, batch pushes and pull requests, and explicitly full or release-update labelled pull requests select every heavy job. Only a full run publishes the check status; ordinary pull requests publish check (fast-only). Superseded pull request runs are cancelled; main pushes keep independent runs. The aggregate accepts skips only for unselected jobs and requires successful fast checks and selected jobs. The aggregate checks that a full lane selected the complete suite. Account usage owns the retained-row, row-polling and app-traffic scenarios once; other account jobs exclude those same target and runtime pairs. Host-managed account and pool-action proofs run as two selected jobs with independent unchanged deadlines, identical runtime preparation and separate retained evidence; their selectors partition the complete previous suite. Signed identity recovery and saved-client diagnostics likewise run as separate selected jobs with identical browser preparation; every scenario and step deadline stays unchanged. Access-editor dispatch and publishing-layout proofs run in their own selected job with the same runtime preparation and evidence retention, so their independent startup costs do not consume the approval and analytics job deadline. The PR fast lane and local pre-push run the same prepared-source preflight: import boundaries, changed-source style, packaged app freshness, strict connector source identity and all root typecheck configurations. The authoring declarations are built before the pool typecheck; full connector runtime proofs keep their separate required jobs. Production builds independently check Worker startup limits. Existing runner configuration only routes selected suites to hosted jobs or Boat bundles. The aggregate requires the selected route to finish successfully; unrelated changes start no bundle. The oversized acquisition upload proof accepts a write-side EPIPE or ECONNRESET during dispatch or response reading only when the independent Worker observation still proves the exact byte limit, cancellation and zero backend calls. The focused app-listener proof uses native HTTP because Node fetch replaces an explicit Host header. The backend must observe the exact requested app host through the held origin. Each production-operation binding proof runs alone with its own database. Signed adoption proofs run two spec files in two test processes with independent scenario identities and identity-scoped SQL gates; every case keeps its own provisioning and cleanup within the unchanged deadlines. The scope runner builds the shared framework once after installing its proof inputs, and each isolated managed run explicitly reuses that unchanged build output. The hosted fallback assigns each identity scenario its own matrix job. Boat bundles retain a private prepared tree per suite because signed proof runners modify source in place. CI assigns each identity scenario its own matrix job so independent Cloud startup costs do not accumulate against one job deadline. A reviewed flake list names each temporary exception and its owning fix. Only final reports with explicit zero unhandled and suite errors can classify a known failure. Physical checks require an accounts-only checksum diff at the unchanged comparison; fixture removal requires a passed body before its exact teardown failure. Missing evidence and every other failure remain blocking. Removing an entry restores blocking without changing the test. Before Vitest starts, binding variants preboot original and changed Alchemy stages with the same canonical origin and configured secrets; timed fixture operations only switch the retained listener, and suite teardown owns both runtimes. App lint reads the immutable host manifest from the exact pinned Git object in its sparse source checkout and then applies manifest carries. It needs no live content API request or host installation. The shared-account health scenario runs in the hosted account gate and identifies status dots by their image role. Physical database fixtures hold runner-owned shared table locks across the clone and both account and receipt baseline reads. Physical database fixtures prepare both Postgres targets before timed scenarios; target readiness inspection is read-only. Container startup finishes before the source snapshot lock is acquired. Physical database fixtures wait for completed initial user setup and saved default-account links before cloning. PostgreSQL commit notifications wake a fresh readiness read; unrelated member work does not delay the baseline. Runner-owned shared table locks cover the clone and both account and receipt baseline reads. The lock scope rolls back before target selection and releases on failure, so normal writes resume after the consistent baseline is captured. Managed Cloud owns retained canonical API and app loopback listeners before creating fixtures. Alchemy binds their private listeners on OS-assigned ports with bind-only collision recovery; each resource's own successful startup announcement supplies its forwarding destination. Original and changed preboot runtimes own distinct app origins. Binding-change proofs for outside-service recovery switch between exactly two prebooted runtimes within the scenario and restore the original in finalization; no switch may launch a replacement. Deliberate restarts keep each canonical origin and replace only its destination. Managed Cloud owns a retained canonical loopback listener before creating fixtures. The production-operation database proxy also binds an OS-assigned loopback listener before allocating its backend and keeps it for the environment scope. Runtime bindings and fixture replies use that actual held port. Proxy startup and cleanup failures retain only closed OS codes and bounded errno values; raw exception messages and nested causes never enter diagnostics. Alchemy binds the private API on an OS-assigned port with bind-only collision recovery, and startup output supplies the actual forwarding destination. Deliberate restarts keep the canonical origin and replace only that destination. Managed Cloud failure evidence distinguishes forwarding request errors from upstream server responses and records closed transport codes and socket reuse without request data or raw exceptions. The gateway drains pending diagnostic writes when its scope closes. Identity and account-recovery evidence retains every runtime variant startup log. Managed Cloud startup retains sanitized Alchemy failure output through stream completion or a bounded quiet period before rejecting readiness. The same cause reaches cloud.log and the startup error; successful readiness has no extra wait. Compiler evidence waits on saved-line notifications through authenticated fixture control until its trace and source digest reach the requested phase, within the scenario's existing deadline. Documentation builds read licensed Latin Geist faces from repository assets through the local font provider, including normal and italic variable weight ranges. CI rebuilds with empty font caches and refuses font requests to the outside service. Live model-probe capture keeps bounded response bodies, including non-JSON failures, with credentials redacted before evidence leaves the browser. The dashboard renderer excludes browser analytics through the framework compiler's client-only boundary; browser event capture and replay keep the same synchronous entry points. The final pool image declares its OAuth completion-receipt capability so upgrading that runtime changes the published image fingerprint. Worker builds place hashed chunks below a chunks directory, leaving exactly one root entry for startup profiling regardless of hash characters. Container reconciliation checks the observed image against the retained publication digest even when input hashes match. Each production release verifies the published manifest and config bytes against their digests, reads the actual platform and OAuth completion capability label, and captures that evidence with its candidate and input fingerprint. Registry manifest and blob paths retain the literal colon in validated OCI digest references. It then verifies the application and every active instance before recording success. Missing publication evidence, failed readiness or drift stops without automatic rollback. Layout evidence queries the visible destination heading and reads its bounds in one browser task, so replacing a lazy fallback cannot leave a detached element handle between selection and measurement. Shared HTTP route declarations initialize as pure metadata factories, so the MCP entry point omits unused route descriptions while HTTP entry points retain the same public contracts."
      metadata { code ".upstream/scripts/e2e-prepare.ts|.upstream/scripts/e2e-check-web.ts|e2e/plans|e2e/pool-removal-intent.mjs|e2e/pool-recipe-checks.mjs|e2e/recipes|.github/workflows/pool-basic-budget.yml|scripts|.upstream/e2e|e2e/automatic-account-rechecks.test.ts|e2e/account-recheck-fixture.ts|e2e/account-recheck-cooldowns.test.ts|e2e/account-recheck-dispatch.test.ts|.upstream/scripts/compiler-cache-proof|assets/fonts/geist|e2e/slack/catalog.mjs|e2e/slack/run.ts|e2e/aws|containers/pool/fetch_releases.py|containers/pool/fetch_sources.py|containers/pool/Dockerfile|.github/workflows/ci.yml|.github/workflows/app-lint.yml|.github/workflows/client-desktop-mac.yml"; endpointHosts "127.0.0.1|localhost|routes-fixture.invalid|synthetic-home.test|synthetic-provider.invalid"; libraries "fumadb-effect|@effect/sql-pglite|@modelcontextprotocol/sdk|node:http|@effect/platform-node|alchemy|esbuild|fontTools.subset|fontTools.ttLib.TTFont|yaml|bun:ffi"; bindings "platform.env.POOL_OPERATIONS=basalt.modelGateway" }
    }
  }
}

model {
  basalt.build -> basalt.toolsGateway.accountRechecks "Verifies bounded account rechecks"
  basalt.build -> basalt.feedback "Redacts retained managed-runtime logs"
}
